# gate/layering.baseline — the forbidden dependency edges that ALREADY EXIST.
#
# This is a debt register, not a permission slip. Every line is a direction
# violation the compiler is currently living with. The gate pins this set
# EXACTLY: a new forbidden edge fails, and a removed one also fails so that
# the person who paid the debt down re-pins the file and the ground cannot be
# quietly given back.
#
# Read the shape of it before adding anything: the backend reaches into AST 20
# times and into SEMA 39 times. `native_backend.zig:6` imports `Sema` itself
# and `codegen.zig` constructs a `Parser` at six live call sites. That is the
# backend recovering meaning it should have been handed. Nothing here is
# endorsed; it is measured so it cannot grow.
#
# FORMAT:  <from-layer>\t<from-file>\t<to-layer>\t<to-file>
#
# Measured 2026-08-22 at idol@40de041c55541c5b86781135858ecc8a7d5c2231.
#   subjects ................ 117 tracked src/*.zig (100% classified)
#   distinct import edges ... 580
#   forbidden edges ......... 75
#     BACKEND -> SEMA ....... 39
#     BACKEND -> AST ........ 20
#     AST -> BACKEND ........  4
#     AST -> SEMA ...........  3
#     IR -> AST .............  3
#     IR -> SEMA ............  2
#     IR -> BACKEND .........  2
#     SEMA -> BACKEND .......  2
#
# Edges inside `test` blocks are counted. In Zig a test-block @import is a
# real compile-time dependency; exempting them would leave the obvious hiding
# place open.
